Article 11 Where a capital contributor makes capital contributions with equity in another company that meets the following conditions, the People's Court shall find that the capital contributor has performed the capital contribution obligation:
(1) The contributed equity is lawfully held by the capital contributor and may be transferred according to law;
(2) The contributed equity is free from any defects in title or encumbrances;
(3) The capital contributor has completed the legal formalities for the transfer of equity;
(4) The contributed equity has been appraised in value according to law.
Where the equity contribution does not meet the requirements of items (1), (2), or (3) of the preceding paragraph, and the company, other shareholders, or creditors of the company request a finding that the capital contributor has not performed the capital contribution obligation, the People's Court shall order the capital contributor to take remedial measures within a specified reasonable period to comply with the aforesaid conditions; if such remedial measures are not taken within the time limit, the People's Court shall find that the capital contributor has not fully performed the capital contribution obligation according to law.
Where the equity contribution does not meet the requirements of item (4) of the first paragraph of this Article, and the company, other shareholders, or creditors of the company request a finding that the capital contributor has not performed the capital contribution obligation, the People's Court shall handle the matter in accordance with the provisions of Article 9 of these Provisions.

Article 12 After the establishment of the company, if the company, a shareholder, or a creditor of the company requests a finding that a relevant shareholder has withdrawn its capital contribution on the ground that such shareholder's conduct falls under any of the following circumstances and harms the company's interests, the People's Court shall uphold such request:
(1) Fabricating false financial accounting statements to inflate profits for distribution;
(2) Transferring out the capital contribution by fabricating creditor-debtor relationships;
(3) Transferring out the capital contribution through connected transactions;
(4) Other acts of withdrawing the capital contribution without going through legal procedures.

Article 22 Where a dispute arises between the parties as to the ownership of equity, and one party requests the People's Court to confirm that it holds the equity, such party shall prove one of the following facts:
(1) It has already made capital contributions to the company or subscribed for capital contributions according to law, and such act does not violate the mandatory provisions of laws and regulations;
(2) It has already acquired the equity in the company by transfer or through other forms of succession, and such act does not violate the mandatory provisions of laws and regulations.
